\section*{Strict expressive gain from witnessed symmetric choice}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that one witnessed symmetric-choice occurrence defines a query beyond ordinary choiceless polynomial time with counting, while giving an answer on every finite input.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
In choiceless polynomial time with counting (CPT), computations use the full hereditarily finite domain without a fixed rank bound. Its witnessed symmetric choice extension (WSC) allows a choice accompanied by automorphism witnesses for the entire input vocabulary, fixing parameters and all previously constructed states. The claim exhibits a fixed vocabulary of eight binary relation symbols and a polynomially bounded CPT+WSC sentence with one occurrence of witnessed symmetric choice such that
\[
 Q\in\operatorname{CPT+WSC},\qquad Q\notin\operatorname{CPT},
\]
where $Q$ is its Boolean query on all finite structures in that vocabulary. The nondefinability assertion ranges over all CPT sentences, not a bounded-rank fragment.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-24), edge and configuration atoms encode compatible states on a charged box. Explicit automorphisms witness symmetric choices and preserve earlier selections; a guarded fixed sentence detects zero charge, while the sparse-support counting argument prevents the original formalism from doing so.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 243; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: one witnessed symmetric-choice occurrence defines a query beyond ordinary choiceless polynomial time with counting, while giving an answer on every finite input.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{ChoicelessPolynomialTime.lean}, \texttt{WitnessedChoice.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CPTSeparation.main}, \texttt{OAI.WitnessedChoice.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

